# Worker + extraction rules

## Routing
- `/api/*` requests run the Worker script first; everything else falls through
  to the static assets binding (`env.ASSETS.fetch(request)`).
- With `@cloudflare/vite-plugin` you do NOT hand-write `assets.directory`.
- SPA fallback: `not_found_handling: "single-page-application"`.

## Extraction endpoint (`POST /api/extract`)
- Cloudflare-native. No third-party AI provider, no API-key secret.
- Rate limit FIRST, using the platform's own rate limiting binding, before
  the body is read and long before any AI call. No stored counters, no KV.
- Accepts `multipart/form-data` with one PDF. Enforce the size cap (8 MB)
  BEFORE reading the file into memory.
- The client's MIME type is ADVISORY — it is a claim, not evidence. It may
  buy a cheap early exit, but the `%PDF-` file signature decides whether the
  bytes are converted, and the `ConversionResult` mimetype must be confirmed
  as `application/pdf` before any converted text reaches the model.
- Conversion: `env.AI.toMarkdown({ name, blob }, { conversionOptions:
  { pdf: { metadata: false } } })`. Metadata is token noise for extraction.
  A `ConversionResult` with `format === "error"` is a user-facing failure.
- There is a ceiling (~50k chars) on converted markdown. Past it, FAIL with a
  friendly message. Never truncate silently: compiling part of a document and
  presenting it as the menu is a quietly wrong answer, which is worse than a
  refusal.
- Extraction model: Workers AI text model supporting JSON Mode, pinned in a
  single constant so it can be swapped. Request structured output via
  `response_format: { type: "json_schema", json_schema: {...} }`.
- Workers AI does NOT guarantee schema compliance; it returns an explicit
  `JSON Mode couldn't be met` error. Handle it as its own failure mode.
- Bindings in wrangler.jsonc: `"ai": { "binding": "AI", "remote": true }`
  (Workers AI has no local emulation, so dev calls the real service) and one
  `ratelimits` entry for the extraction route.
- Provider concerns live behind one port interface so the model can change.
- Extraction output uses per-menu vocabulary; never coerce into the demo
  enums. It must NOT include any allergen/gluten-free field, ever.
- Validate deterministically. Reject invalid or model-invented output;
  never silently repair. Require >= 4 valid items AND enough attribute
  variety that at least one filter question clears ASK_COST.
- SOURCE GROUNDING. Vocabulary closure proves only that the model agrees with
  itself — it writes both the option list and the attributes. Anything
  load-bearing is checked against the converted markdown, which is the one
  input to this step the model did not write. A model-generated value is
  never treated as grounded because it matches another model-generated field.
  - Every item carries quoted `evidence` from the document. The evidence must
    appear in the markdown, the item's name must appear inside the evidence,
    and evidence is length-capped so quoting the whole document proves
    nothing.
  - `vegetarian` / `vegan` are only true when the document itself marks them
    in that item's evidence. Never infer either from ingredients. Dietary
    status is a hard constraint on the universe, so an ungrounded claim gets
    the item rejected, not downgraded.
- Portion collapse requires more than a matching normalised name: every
  attribute the engine decides on must agree too. A shared base name over
  differing attributes means two products, and merging them would erase a
  real branch of the decision tree.
- Never log, persist, or write to disk any uploaded file or derived content.
- The engine that consumes the result stays deterministic. Extraction is the
  only probabilistic step in the whole app.

## Telemetry (only if built)
- Fire-and-forget via `navigator.sendBeacon`; never block the UI.
- No cookies, no user IDs, no IP storage, no free text. Event name + numeric
  fields only. Failures silently dropped.
